From 3b1ea1b50e11bbd241d8edce1fe045e0b5ddefa7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dispatch Sandbox <jax1313@outlook.com>
Date: Sat, 12 Sep 2026 20:27:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 10/12] Wire the capacity engine into production, and make
 appointment times readable

dispatch/capacity.py is 1,861 lines with 1,000 lines of tests against it, and
none of it could be reached from the running program: DynamicCapacity was never
constructed outside tests/, there was no table a profile could live in, and the
one production call into scoring is portal/helpers.py -> score_load(data) with
no capacity -- whose own docstring says that without one "the result is exactly
what it has always been". Dead code that is well tested is the hard case: it
raised the gated coverage figure while evaluating nothing.

Wired, not reserved. docs/MANAGER.md is this repository's precedent for formally
reserving a capability and it is explicit about what it reserves -- something
"named in planning and never built". That does not reach working code, and the
Purpose Statement's second verb is Evaluate Possibilities. What was missing was
one table wide: capacity_profiles, one row per truck, sections as JSON because
they are a document. Utilization is deliberately not persisted -- used_weight_lbs
describes what is on the trailer now, and a saved one comes back as a claim
about today made from last week's freight.

An unprofiled truck is UNCONFIGURED on the load page, never "probably fine".
Assuming a 53-foot dry van is how freight gets accepted onto a trailer that
cannot carry it.

Wiring it surfaced why it could never have worked as it stood.
parse_operational_timestamp rejects a timestamp with no offset -- correctly: "a
pickup window in an unstated zone is genuinely ambiguous, and guessing costs a
load" -- and the caller raises a SEVERITY_BLOCKING finding. The load form's own
placeholder was `YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM`. A naive timestamp. Every load entered in the
documented format would have produced a blocking finding, and the conclusion a
person draws is that the capacity engine refuses everything.

The same gap had a quieter effect: get_load_calendar() selected a month by
string prefix on a free-text field, so a load typed `9/14/2026 08:00` -- what a
US dispatcher writes by hand, accepted without complaint by a text input -- was
silently absent from the calendar. No error, no warning, no row. And the day was
sliced off the raw string, so a delivery at 2026-09-15T01:00Z filed on the 15th
when in Eastern time it is the evening of the 14th.

dispatch/timestamps.py resolves both without weakening the engine's rule: the
operator's zone is configuration (DISPATCH_OPERATING_TIMEZONE), and attaching it
to a value typed without one is not guessing -- a dispatcher who types 08:00
means eight in the morning where they are. Attaching UTC would be the guess. A
value that already carries an offset is stored untouched, and one that cannot be
read is kept exactly as typed and reported, never rewritten or dropped.

portal/models/conflict.py had a fourth hand-rolled datetime parser whose formats
carried no offset, so it would have silently stopped detecting every conflict.
It now uses the one parser, and compares instants: overlap arithmetic on naive
local times is how a conflict is missed by four hours.

And a |local_time filter, because every timestamp in this program was rendered
raw -- 2026-08-12T14:00:00-04:00 on the rate confirmation a broker receives and
on the driver's own screen. That is a 70 MPH failure: the driver is moving,
tired and has one hand, and an ISO string makes him do arithmetic to find out
when the appointment is.
